1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,063
[THE CHALLENGE] Hello everyone, and respected judges! Today, modern applications are built using hundreds of open-source packages across npm, Python, Go, and GitHub workflows.

2
00:00:09,063 --> 00:00:18,126
[THE CHALLENGE] While open-source libraries help us build products faster, managing their security has become extremely complex.

3
00:00:18,126 --> 00:00:27,190
[THE CHALLENGE] In large codebases, applications often contain multiple levels of nested dependencies.

4
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:36,253
[THE CHALLENGE] The real challenge for engineering teams is knowing: Which vulnerabilities are actually reachable in production code, and which ones are never called?

5
00:00:36,253 --> 00:00:45,316
[THE CHALLENGE] Without this reachability context, developers spend weeks investigating alerts or risk breaking live backend APIs during package upgrades.

6
00:00:45,316 --> 00:00:54,380
[THE CHALLENGE] This is why modern software security requires a deeper, code-aware approach.

7
00:00:54,980 --> 00:01:04,011
[PROPOSED SOLUTION] To address this challenge, we created SupplyGraph.

8
00:01:04,011 --> 00:01:13,043
[PROPOSED SOLUTION] SupplyGraph is an Application Security Posture Management platform designed to give developers complete visibility into their software supply chain.

9
00:01:13,043 --> 00:01:22,075
[PROPOSED SOLUTION] Instead of treating dependencies as flat isolated lists, SupplyGraph analyzes project call paths and builds an interactive visual attack graph.

10
00:01:22,075 --> 00:01:31,106
[PROPOSED SOLUTION] It connects your code from user-facing entry points all the way down to deeply nested libraries.

11
00:01:31,106 --> 00:01:40,138
[PROPOSED SOLUTION] It clearly verifies whether an execution path actually reaches a vulnerable component.

12
00:01:40,138 --> 00:01:49,170
[PROPOSED SOLUTION] When an issue is identified, SupplyGraph provides safe, line-by-line code patches that preserve application functionality, while testing untrusted packages inside an isolated sandbox.

13
00:01:49,770 --> 00:02:01,962
[CORE FEATURES] Let us look at four core capabilities of SupplyGraph:

14
00:02:01,962 --> 00:02:14,154
[CORE FEATURES] First is the Attack Graph. Powered by graph traversal algorithms, it visually maps the relationship between code and libraries, showing exact reachability paths.

15
00:02:14,153 --> 00:02:26,346
[CORE FEATURES] Second is the Blast Radius Score. It calculates a clear risk metric from 0 to 100, helping teams understand potential operational impact.

16
00:02:26,346 --> 00:02:38,538
[CORE FEATURES] Third is the Autonomous Executive AI. It summarizes technical vulnerability data into actionable briefings and prioritized containment steps.

17
00:02:38,538 --> 00:02:50,730
[CORE FEATURES] And fourth is the CycloneDX SBOM. It generates standard Software Bill of Materials documents with deterministic SHA-256 cryptographic verification for compliance.

18
00:02:51,330 --> 00:03:01,770
[DEMO SCENARIOS] To demonstrate these capabilities, SupplyGraph includes five preloaded real-world scenarios:

19
00:03:01,770 --> 00:03:12,210
[DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario A illustrates a transitive Log4j call path, mapping how an incoming request travels through service layers to a vulnerable logging library.

20
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:22,650
[DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario B examines Prototype Pollution, showing how improper object merging can be safely identified and corrected.

21
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:33,090
[DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario C analyzes insecure Python pickle deserialization, demonstrating AST-level detection before code execution.

22
00:03:33,090 --> 00:03:43,530
[DEMO SCENARIOS] Scenario D audits GitHub Actions workflows, ensuring runners and third-party actions use immutable commit hashes.

23
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:53,969
[DEMO SCENARIOS] And Scenario E tests lifecycle scripts, demonstrating how package installation hooks are monitored and controlled.

24
00:03:54,570 --> 00:04:07,413
[REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] Now let us examine a real-world open-source repository: listmonk by Kailash Nadh.

25
00:04:07,413 --> 00:04:20,256
[REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] When scanning a multi-module repository like this, SupplyGraph indexes all 35 submodules and manifests, providing a unified inventory across frontend, backend, and workflows.

26
00:04:20,256 --> 00:04:33,100
[REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] It identifies 21 findings across direct and transitive dependencies, computing a contextual Blast Radius score of 60 out of 100.

27
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:45,943
[REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] With the 'Fix with AI' feature, SupplyGraph generates evidence-based, non-breaking remediation prompts specifically structured for AI coding agents like Antigravity, Cursor, and Copilot.

28
00:04:45,943 --> 00:04:58,786
[REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] In the AI Code Reviewer, developers inspect surgical unified git diffs that correct specific vulnerable lines rather than forcing broad version jumps.

29
00:04:58,786 --> 00:05:11,629
[REAL PROJECT WALKTHROUGH] And in our Detonation Sandbox, lifecycle scripts run within an isolated container where unauthorized network connections and file modifications are safely contained.
